00:27:58.000 --> 00:28:53.000
their innocence. He was also the first person to publicly suggest the serial killer was responsible for the deaths of Mitra, Zivana and Lubica long before police had suggested the same. Additionally, Taneski speculated over the way in which Zivana Temelkowska had been abducted, specifying that she may have been coaxed from her house by false radio reports that her son had been involved in an accident. As a reporter, Taneski regularly submitted stories for inclusion on local radio news bulletins, ensuring he had the means to issue a false statement for broadcast. Suspicions directed towards Taneski were fully ignited when police noticed the seemingly offhand detail in an article he wrote for Nova Macedonia about Lubica Larkowska's murder.

34
00:28:53.000 --> 00:29:46.000
The monstrous murderer brutally tortured the helpless woman, killing her by strangling her with a telephone cord. The fact that a telephone cord was used as the killer's signature weapon was not publicly known at the time and had been strategically withheld by police to maintain the integrity of their investigation, a police spokesperson said. We read Taneski's stories and they made us suspicious. He knew too much. Following his arrest, Taneski insisted he didn't know any of the victims personally, a remark that only served to further arouse police suspicion. They were fully aware that Taneski lived in the same close-knit neighborhood as the murdered women and that each had been an associate of his late mother. Furthermore, Lubica Larkowska had

